From b60246c9cb0ccca3e37c7ebb6611aa99af0ff184 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:19:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 53/91] moe-stream : the expert cache released for a long
 prompt, the waves of all layers from one shared pool (SiegeLadder P11,
 LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_P11=N, off by default) - a long prompt's -ub is held by VRAM
 (the compute buffer grows ~0.68 MiB per token of -ub at 32K), and every
 ubatch copies ~70% of the experts of every layer to the card (SiegeLadder
 runs.md 4.83: 69 MB per token at -ub 1536, 21 MB at 6144). While P1's head
 yield is out, the manager now releases the expert cache buffer as P1 releases
 the head (p11_enter, after waiting until no load writes a slot and the upload
 stream has landed), and points every streamed layer's cache tensors into one
 pool of N slots ([ne0, ne1, N] per weight, all layers sharing it); a layer's
 slot table starts empty at its first wave (wave_ids), since the pool held
 other layers' experts in between - safe because the layers run one after
 another and each wave's CPU split follows a synchronize of the compute
 stream. While it is active every ubatch takes the wave path (a plan that fits
 one pass runs as two waves): the remap path may run while the card still
 reads the pool (the mailbox) and its table would be stale. p11_leave (in
 p1_leave, after the CUDA pool is trimmed and before the head comes back)
 allocates the cache again, empty. A wave holds N/2 experts instead of 8, so
 the per-wave passes (clamp, swiglu, ids, quantize - more than the expert
 GEMMs themselves at 17 slots, runs.md 4.83) fall 4x at N = 64. SiegeLadder
 runs.md 4.85, consultant 30K (17 slots): -ub 1536 182.9 tok/s vs 157.9
 (+16%); -ub 6144 with the tier at 44 GiB (the larger CUDA_Host inputs need
 room in the shared WDDM budget) 276.9 tok/s, 107 s vs 187.5 (+75%), 0.47 TB
 from the drive vs 1.41, waves 2 480 vs 38 520; decode after the document
 unchanged. Not bit-exact against the per-layer cache (the waves' composition
 changes MMQ's split, as the cache state already does): the answers diverge
 after 190 / 298 characters, the base against itself after 18-79. The default
 path is unchanged (o30: counters equal to the unit, text = trace)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
